    "Leaf wax-mimetic silk nanoparticle for fungicide delivery: A bioinspired approach to enhance foliar adhesion and retention": 2,
    "Muscle disorders including degenerative conditions such as sarcopenia remain a major clinical challenge due to the lack of effective therapeutic options. To discover novel agents that promote muscle growth and regeneration we targeted myostatin a well-characterized negative regulator of muscle growth. Using an in silico screening approach we explored a natural compounds database to identify molecules with potential binding affinity for myostatin. Selected candidates were subsequently evaluated for their myogenic effects in murine myoblasts (C2C12) and human satellite cells. Treatment with Compound A led to a significant increase in myotube diameter and creatine kinase activity along with upregulated expression of key myogenic markers (MyoD Myogenin and Myosin heavy chain). These findings suggest that this identified natural compound may serve as a promising therapeutic agent for enhancing muscle growth and regeneration by inhibiting myostatin activity.": 2,
    "Plant-based proteins are considered excellent nutritional resources due to their low cost and diverse functional properties. Sesame meal (SM)―a by-product from oil-extracted sesame seeds―is particularly rich in crude protein (35-40%) and sulfur-containing amino acids such as methionine and cysteine suitable for meat analogs. Sesame meal protein isolate (SMPI; crude protein content 86.82%) exhibited superior gel-forming ability compared to soy protein isolate (SPI) as evidenced by a 3.22-fold higher storage modulus and a 0.37-fold lower tan δ during temperature ramping. Improved rheology led to enhanced texture with the SMPI meat analog showing 3.0- 2.5- and 5.5-fold increases in compressive force resilience and cutting strength respectively. The stronger molecular interactions within the SMPI matrix also contributed to improved strand stability and fiber alignment during 3D printing facilitating the formation of a meat-like structure. These findings suggest that SMPI demonstrates substantial potential as a protein source for plant-based meat analog production particularly in applications requiring structural integrity and printability.": 2,
    "The low biodegradability and high cytotoxicity of synthetic emulsifiers have raised concerns in the food industry leading to an increasing demand for natural emulsifiers such as plant-derived proteins. Sesame meal protein isolate (SMPI) a by-product of sesame oil production represents a sustainable source of functional protein. In this study SMPI was enzymatically hydrolyzed using papain (SMPI-PP) to enhance its emulsifying functionality. The enzymatic treatment markedly improved the solubility and surface hydrophobicity of SMPI thereby facilitating interfacial adsorption. Compared to untreated SMPI SMPI-PP exhibited 1.14- and 2.24-fold higher emulsifying activity index (EAI) and emulsion stability index (ESI) respectively. Its emulsifying properties were also comparable to or superior to those of whey protein isolate (WPI). Oil-in-water nanoemulsions stabilized with SMPI-PP showed similar initial droplet sizes to those of WPI but after 28 days of storage at 40 °C SMPI-PP emulsions maintained smaller droplet sizes (268.17 nm) than WPI emulsions (353.58 nm) and exhibited a lower creaming index (CI). These findings highlight the potential of enzymatically modified SMPI as an effective and sustainable natural emulsifier for food applications.": 2,
    "The rapid increase in global population and the corresponding surge in protein demand have raised significant challenges regarding protein supply and food security. Consequently increasing numbers of research on plant-based proteins as meat analogs has been conducted. Soy protein isolate (SPI) has been the most extensively studied due to its gelling ability and water holding capacity making it suitable for meat analogs [1]. However soy can be an allergenic food restricting its consumption among certain consumer groups and it is often unable to fully replicate the diverse textures flavors and nutritional characteristics of real meat [1]. Developing new plant-based protein sources is essential for offering a viable alternative or complement to current legume-based proteins.Sesame meal a by-product of sesame oil extraction has been underestimated frequently discarded or designated for animal feed. However sesame meal has a high crude protein content (50%) establishing it as a promising protein source [2]. Upcycling sesame meal into sesame meal protein isolate (SMPI) not only addresses environmental concerns but also creates new economic opportunities as a sustainable food ingredient. This study compares the rheological physicochemical and textural properties of meat analogs produced using SMPI and SPI. Results suggest SMPI presents a viable alternative or supplement to SPI in meat analog production indicating potential for further exploration in this field.": 2,
    "A growing demand for clean-label products has increased the need for plant-based protein emulsifiers in the food industry. However the poor solubility and limited functionality of plant proteins hinder their applications as emulsifiers. Sesame meal protein isolate (SMPI) derived from agricultural by-products can be a promising candidate. In this study SMPI was treated with papain (SMPI-PP) to investigate the enhancement of its emulsifying performance. The enzymatic treatment increased the solubility and surface hydrophobicity of SMPI. SMPI-PP showed a 1.14-fold and 1.19-fold increase in emulsifying activity index (EAI) and 2.24-fold and comparable increase in emulsion stability index (ESI) compared to untreated SMPI and whey protein isolate (WPI) respectively. O/W nanoemulsions prepared SMPI- PP and WPI showed similar initial droplet sizes. However after 7 days of storage at 40℃ SMPI-PP emulsion maintained smaller droplet size (254.90 nm) compared to WPI (365.43 nm). SMPI-PP emulsions showed a 2-fold lower creaming index (CI) than WPI emulsions. These findings suggest the potential of enzymatically modified SMPI as an effective natural emulsifier for food applications.": 2,
    "Plant-based proteins are valued for their affordability and functional properties. Sesame meal (SM) a by-product of oil-extracted sesame seeds contains high crude protein content (3540%) and sulfur-containing amino acids such as methionine and cysteine making it suitable for meat analogs. Sesame meal protein isolate (SMPI; crude protein content 86.82%) extracted from SM exhibited superior gel-forming ability compared to soy protein isolate (SPI) with a 3.22-fold higher storage modulus and 0.37-fold lower tan δ under programmed temperature ramping indicating a more elastic solid-like network. SMPI exhibited enhanced rheological properties leading to improved texture in model meat analog (MMA) relative to SPI as demonstrated by 3.0- 2.5- and 5.5-fold higher compressive force resilience and cutting strength respectively. The increased proportion of water with the highest mobility and prolonged T₂ relaxation time observed in the LF-NMR T₂ profiles of SMPI MMA indicates that enhanced proteinprotein interactions contribute to water release. Protein inhibitor treatment disrupted the network structure of both SMPI and SPI MMAs revealing that hydrophobic and hydrogen bonds were the primary interactions forming the gel network while disulfide bonds appeared to contribute to network reinforcement. These findings highlight SMPI as a sustainable protein source for plant-based meat analogs that require structural integrity and robust texture.": 2,
    "A GPS-Based Autonomous Navigation Framework for Shuttle Transportation Robots in Orchard Environments": 2,
    "A biopolyurethane foam was developed using a polyol synthesized from neem oil a plantbased raw material. The neem oil-based polyol (NOP) was synthesized by adding calcium oxide as a catalyst to a mixture of neem oil and glycerol followed by stirring at 220 °C for 3 h. The synthesized NOP was blended with a polyester polyol and then reacted with methylene diphenyl diisocyanate (MDI) to form polyurethane where the equivalent ratio of polyol to MDI was set at 1.0:1.1. Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR) analysis of the NOP showed a characteristic hydroxyl peak at 3442.85 cm¹ confirming the conversion of neem oil triglycerides into polyols. The acid values of neem oil and the synthesized NOP were 6.02 mg KOH/g and 2.96 mg KOH/g respectively. The compressive and tensile strengths of the biopolyurethane prepared from NOP were 0.059 ± 0.010 MPa and 0.020 ± 0.010 MPa respectively. These results demonstrate the potential of NOP as an eco-friendly alternative material for the synthesis of biopolyurethane foam. ": 2,
    "Leuconostoc lactis DMLL10 from kimchi was selected as a potential starter based on its safety and technological properties. To investigate the impact of L. lactis DMLL10 strain on kimchi fermentation changes in microbial communities organic acids free amino acids and volatile compounds were monitored over the fermentation period. Kimchi without starter strain and with L. mesenteroides were used as the control. We checked whether fermentation was proceeding well by measuring the viable cell count pH and acidity and fermentation progressed well in all three sample groups. Furthermore the added starter culture became dominant as fermentation progressed. The kimchi fermented with L. lactis DMLL10 strain showed distinct profiles of organic acids amino acids and volatile compounds compared to both the control and the L. mesenteroides inoculated kimchi. Notably the levels of umami-related compounds such as aspartic acid and glutamic acid were significantly higher. These findings demonstrate that the sensory characteristics of fermentation products can vary depending on the starter strain used. This highlights the importance of accurately analyzing strain- or species-specific differences to develop fermented foods that meet consumer preferences.Funding: RS-2022-IP322014": 2
